    "body": "Credit: AF-KLM\nBy Alex Lennane\n5 November 2026\nKLM plans to consolidate cargo handling at Schiphol to accommodate additional freight without hiring more staff, as AI supply chains and disruption to ocean shipping strengthen parent Air France-KLM’s cargo business.\nThe changes form part of a wider restructuring, announced today, aimed at improving KLM’s finances and its ability to fund investment.\nFreight carried on passenger aircraft and freighters will be handled in KLM’s own warehouse at Schiphol, said the airline, adding that the consolidation would help attract more transfer freight and prepare for a new cargo terminal, scheduled for completion in 2035.\n“The increased cargo volumes will ultimately be handled without hiring additional employees,” KLM said.\nIt plans to achieve this through “simpler processes, greater automation and deploying employees across different activities”. It also intends to expand services for customers requiring additional support, although it gave no further detail.\nThe announcement follows conversations at last week’s TIACA Air Cargo Forum in Miami, where The Loadstar heard that AI infrastructure shipments were contributing to transit cargo flows through Amsterdam.\nAir France-KLM’s third-quarter results provided evidence of the wider demand trend. Group cargo revenue increased 25.2% year on year, to €677m ($760m), while cargo traffic revenue rose 24.9%, to €566m.\nThe group attributed the growth to sea-to-air modal shift amid disruption around the Suez Canal and Strait of Hormuz, alongside AI-related demand.\n“The surge in air freight demand driven by AI-related supply chains tightened global market conditions,” it said.\nThis helped the group win additional volumes and full-freighter charter business, supporting yields.\nKLM’s cargo traffic, measured in revenue tonne-km, rose 15.7% against capacity growth of 4.5%. Its cargo load factor increased 5.2 percentage points, to 53.9%.\nAcross Air France-KLM, tonnage rose 5%, to 236,000 tonnes. Revenue per available tonne-km increased 21.9% at constant currency, with demand outpacing capacity growth. Scheduled and unscheduled maintenance constrained freighter availability.\nSchiphol’s latest monthly figures show September cargo volumes of 127,750 tonnes, up 8% year on year, with growth of 10% over the first nine months. Freighters accounted for 54% of September tonnage, and belly cargo 46%.\nDespite cargo’s performance, KLM said its overall profitability remained insufficient to support investment. Third-quarter adjusted operating profit rose to €458m, from a restated €352m a year earlier.\n“Fuel prices continue to put pressure on our results, our cost base remains too high,” said KLM CEO Marjan Rintel.\nThe Dutch carrier is seeking a further €1bn improvement in annual earnings, taking its total target to €1.4bn, and at least an 8% structural productivity increase by 2028.\nThe wider programme involves around 5,000 positions whose work has been, or will be, reorganised, including transfers to external providers and the parent group. Productivity improvements are expected to result in approximately 1,000 fewer positions at KLM.\nThe airline expects to limit the impact through natural attrition, voluntary arrangements, and internal transfers.",
